{
  "title": "I don't feel well",
  "title_target": "אֲנִי לֹא מַרְגִּישׁ טוֹב",
  "goal": "Describe symptoms, name body parts and ask for medicine — using a typically Hebrew pattern: pain that \"happens to you\", not pain you have.",
  "bekal": [
    "naming the body part that hurts",
    "describing symptoms at the pharmacy or to a doctor",
    "understanding simple advice"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "שִׂיחָה — בְּבֵית מִרְקַחַת",
      "intro": "Budi walks into a pharmacy looking worn out. The pharmacist, Tamar, greets him.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "שָׁלוֹם, אֵיךְ אֲנִי יְכוֹלָה לַעֲזֹר?",
          "baca": "shalom, eikh ani yekholah la'azor?",
          "id": "Hi, how can I help?",
          "literal": "hello, how I can (f) to-help?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "אֲנִי לֹא מַרְגִּישׁ טוֹב. כּוֹאֵב לִי הָרֹאשׁ.",
          "baca": "ani lo margish tov. ko'ev li harosh.",
          "id": "I don't feel well. I have a headache.",
          "literal": "I not feel good. hurts to-me the-head."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "מִמָּתַי? יֵשׁ לְךָ חֹם?",
          "baca": "mimatai? yesh lekha khom?",
          "id": "Since when? Do you have a fever?",
          "literal": "from-when? there-is to-you heat?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "מֵאֶתְמוֹל. אֲנִי חוֹשֵׁב שֶׁכֵּן, קְצָת.",
          "baca": "me'etmol. ani khoshev sheken, ktsat.",
          "id": "Since yesterday. I think so, a little.",
          "literal": "from-yesterday. I think that-yes, a-little."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "כּוֹאֵב לְךָ גַּם הַגָּרוֹן?",
          "baca": "ko'ev lekha gam hagaron?",
          "id": "Does your throat hurt too?",
          "literal": "hurts to-you also the-throat?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "כֵּן, וְקָשֶׁה לִי לִבְלֹעַ.",
          "baca": "ken, vekasheh li livlo'a.",
          "id": "Yes, and it's hard to swallow.",
          "literal": "yes, and-hard to-me to-swallow."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "כַּנִּרְאֶה שַׁפַּעַת. יֵשׁ לְךָ אֲלֶרְגְיָה לְמַשֶּׁהוּ?",
          "baca": "kanir'eh shapa'at. yesh lekha alergyah lemashehu?",
          "id": "Probably the flu. Are you allergic to anything?",
          "literal": "apparently flu. there-is to-you allergy to-something?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "לֹא, אֵין לִי.",
          "baca": "lo, ein li.",
          "id": "No, I'm not.",
          "literal": "no, there-isn't to-me."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "קַח אֶת זֶה, כַּדּוּר כָּל שֵׁשׁ שָׁעוֹת.",
          "baca": "kakh et zeh, kadur kol shesh sha'ot.",
          "id": "Take this, one tablet every six hours.",
          "literal": "take (obj) this, tablet every six hours."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "לִפְנֵי הָאֹכֶל אוֹ אַחֲרֵי?",
          "baca": "lifnei ha'okhel o akharei?",
          "id": "Before meals or after?",
          "literal": "before the-meal or after?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "אַחֲרֵי. וְתִשְׁתֶּה הַרְבֵּה מַיִם וְתָנוּחַ.",
          "baca": "akharei. vetishteh harbeh mayim vetanu'akh.",
          "id": "After. And drink lots of water and rest.",
          "literal": "after. and-you-will-drink much water and-you-will-rest."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "תּוֹדָה. וְאִם לֹא יַעֲבֹר?",
          "baca": "todah. ve'im lo ya'avor?",
          "id": "Thanks. And if it doesn't go away?",
          "literal": "thanks. and-if not will-pass?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "תָּמָר:",
          "text": "אָז לֵךְ לְרוֹפֵא. תִּרְגִּישׁ טוֹב!",
          "baca": "az lekh lerofe. targish tov!",
          "id": "Then see a doctor. Feel better!",
          "literal": "so go to-doctor. you-will-feel good!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **כּוֹאֵב לִי הָרֹאשׁ** literally means **\"the head hurts to me\"** — the body part is the subject, not you. English \"my head hurts\" is close; \"I have a headache\" is far off. Think \"my head hurts\" and you're nearly there.",
        "**כּוֹאֵב changes form to match the body part**, because it's a verb: כּוֹאֵב לִי הָרֹאשׁ (head, m), כּוֹאֶבֶת לִי הַבֶּטֶן (stomach, f), כּוֹאֲבוֹת לִי הָרַגְלַיִם (legs, pl). The body part decides, not you.",
        "**Paired body parts are feminine** — יָד (hand), רֶגֶל (leg), עַיִן (eye), אֹזֶן (ear) — and their plural uses the pair form ־ַיִם: יָדַיִם, רַגְלַיִם, עֵינַיִם, אָזְנַיִם. You saw this rule in units 11 and 17; now the reason is complete.",
        "**מַרְגִּישׁ** (feel) works for both physical state and mood: אֲנִי מַרְגִּישׁ טוֹב / רַע / עָיֵף. Feminine: מַרְגִּישָׁה.",
        "**קָשֶׁה לִי לְ...** = \"it's hard for me to...\". A very useful pattern that reaches far beyond health: קָשֶׁה לִי לְדַבֵּר עִבְרִית, קָשֶׁה לִי לָקוּם מֻקְדָּם. The opposite: קַל לִי לְ...",
        "**כַּנִּרְאֶה** (*kanir'eh*) = \"apparently, it seems\". A word that lets you guess without committing — very handy for a learner who isn't sure yet.",
        "**תִּשְׁתֶּה וְתָנוּחַ** uses the **future as a gentle instruction** — the politest way to give advice in Hebrew, softer than a direct imperative. Like English \"you'll want to drink lots of water\" instead of \"drink!\".",
        "**תִּרְגִּישׁ טוֹב!** (\"may you feel good\") is the standard thing to say to someone who's sick. To a woman: תַּרְגִּישִׁי טוֹב. A more formal equivalent: רְפוּאָה שְׁלֵמָה (\"a complete recovery\")."
      ],
